# LLM mode

@lede
A deterministic, token-minimized rendering of a Brief document, intended
for inclusion in LLM prompts.
@end

## Why a separate target

A document for human readers is not the same artifact as a document for
an LLM consumer. Humans benefit from generous whitespace, decorative
syntax, and stable formatting. LLMs pay for every token and benefit from
density.

Markdown's response is "render it the same way and let the LLM cope."
Brief's response is to compile the same source twice, once for each
audience, and to make the LLM-mode rendering the durable advantage.

```bash
brief compile doc.brf --target=llm
brief compile doc.brf --target=llm --report-tokens
brief compile doc.brf --target=llm -w        # writes doc.txt
```

## Transformation rules

The reference compiler applies the following rules in `--target=llm`:

@dl
Comments
: Stripped entirely. Both `//` and `/* */` forms.
Blank lines
: Collapsed to a single newline.
Trailing whitespace
: Stripped on every line.
Indentation
: Normalized to one space per nesting level (instead of the source's
  two spaces).
Heading sigils
: Preserved. LLMs use them as structural cues.
Emphasis markers
: Preserved by default. `--strip-emphasis` removes them; text content
  survives.
Tables
: Compact pipe form. Header row is followed by `|---|` *only* if
  `--keep-table-rule` is passed.
Code blocks
: Verbatim, fences preserved — except for languages in the
  minification allowlist. See @ref[llm-mode/code-minification.brf](Code-block minification).
Shortcodes
: Render via `template_llm` if defined, or to a compact built-in form.
Backslash escapes
: Resolved to their literal characters.
Frontmatter
: Stripped by default. `--keep-metadata` overrides.
@end

## Built-in shortcode renderings

@t
| Source | LLM-mode output
| `@link[text](url)` | `[text](url)`
| `@image(src, alt)` | `[image: alt]`
| `@kbd[Ctrl+C]` | `Ctrl+C`
| `@callout(kind: warning) ... @end` | `[!warning] ... [/!]`
| `@math[x^2]` | `$x^2$`
| `@footnote[body]` | `[^N]` inline, `[^N]: body` appended at end of doc
| `@ref[path#anchor](text)` | `text` — the cross-document link is dropped
| `@sub[2]` | `_2`
| `@sup[2]` | `^2`

## Determinism

Same input → byte-identical output. The rules above are total: every
construct has a deterministic LLM-mode rendering. There is no "best
effort" path.

This matters because it lets you:

- Cache the rendered output and feed it to many model calls.
- Compare token counts of two phrasings reliably.
- Diff the rendered prompt across changes to see exactly what the LLM
  will see differently.

## What LLM mode is not

LLM mode is *lossy by design*. There is no inverse transformation back to
source. If you want a human-readable artifact, use `--target=html`. If
you want a machine-readable representation, use `--target=json`.

LLM mode is not stable surface. The compiler may improve token
efficiency across releases. If you depend on exact byte sequences, pin
a specific compiler version.

## Reach for it when

@dl
Building agent prompts
: A prompt that loads a document into context pays per token on every
  call. LLM mode is the right rendering.
Cost-budgeting documentation
: `--report-tokens` plus a CI gate keeps the cl100k token count within a
  budget you set for your prompt corpus.
Round-tripping
: You don't. LLM mode is one-way. Keep the `.brf` source and re-compile.
@end

## Avoid it when

The audience is human. The HTML target is faster to read, and "minified"
prose is hostile to people. LLM mode is *only* worth it when the consumer
is a model.
